; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; The evidence pack: what one deployment hands over when it answers for a span of time rather than
; for one response. A receipt attests a response; a pack attests that the responses inside a window
; are all of them, and it carries the two chain endpoints that let a reader with no network and none
; of our code establish that for themselves.
;
; Nothing in this repository assembles a pack today. This file does not pretend to be a description
; of a thing that runs: it is the layout a generator has to be written against, and the layout a
; verifier can be written against now. That order is deliberate. A third party's verdict is read out
; of this container, so the container is public whether or not the tool that fills it is.
;
; Section 5.2 of `docs/receipt-spec.md` states the walk this container is built for and publishes the
; frames as byte images in `packages/fixtures/data/chain-v1.json`. Where a sentence here and those
; two disagree about a frame, they are the authority and this file is wrong.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

Ashaveri-Pack = #6.18(COSE_Sign1-Pack-COSE)

; The envelope is the receipt's, unchanged but for the content type: COSE_Sign1 per RFC 9052 section
; 4.2, encoded per RFC 8949 with Core Deterministic Encoding, so a stranger who rebuilds a pack from
; this file and the receipts inside it gets the same bytes back out and signs, or refuses, the
; document the issuer made.
COSE_Sign1-Pack-COSE = [
    protected: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Pack-Protected-Header,
    unprotected: { * any => any }, ; the map that carries no claim; see the note below the manifest
    payload: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Pack-Manifest,
    signature: bstr .size 64
]

; The same three labels the receipt signs with, and a typ that differs because the two documents come
; from one key: a reader who took a pack for a receipt would verify a signature over a manifest and
; report it as an attestation about a single response, which is the one mistake a content type exists
; to prevent and it is not recoverable afterwards, because both documents pass.
Ashaveri-Pack-Protected-Header = {
    1: -8,                    ; alg: EdDSA (Ed25519)
    4: bstr .size 32,         ; kid: sha256(public_key)
    3: "ashaveri/pack",       ; typ
}

; The signed manifest, and the only thing here a verifier of a pack has to believe before it starts
; walking. Each member passed one test: a reader of the walk needs it, and cannot get it out of the
; items. That test is why `span`, `chain` and `duty` are here and why the store's window counts, its
; policy and its retirement history are not — those describe the deployment's retention state, which
; a retention artifact states and this container carries nothing of. A second copy of a number the
; deployment already publishes elsewhere is the thing that drifts.
;
; `v: 1` is one version, and a version here says what the fields attest rather than which software
; wrote them, as it does in `receipt.cddl`. A second version arrives the day a reader would draw a
; different conclusion from the same bytes: an added member a verifier may ignore is that day, and an
; added member a verifier must not miss is that day too, because silence must not read as compliance.
; Nothing about the duty mapping, the framing or the envelope is expected to change without one.
Ashaveri-Pack-Manifest = {
    v: 1,                     ; format version
    at: int,                  ; unix seconds, the instant assembly began
    span: PackSpan,
    chain: PackChain,
    duty: PackDuty,
    items: [+ PackItem],      ; at least one; the reason is below
}

; Stamped before the reads rather than after them, because a slow store must not be able to date a
; pack later than the window it describes: `held` is measured against this instant and the span is
; served as of it, so a stamp taken afterwards would move the answer without moving the evidence.
;
; The span is half-open — `from` included, `to` excluded — because that is the bound the store serves
; by: a receipt issued at exactly the end stamp belongs to the next pack, not this one, and a format
; that left that out would let two packs both claim it or neither. A reader needs both bounds and not
; only the walk. The walk shows that nothing between the first item and the last is missing; the span
; is what shows the reader whether the run the walk covers is the period they asked for.
PackSpan = {
    from: int,                ; unix seconds, included
    to: int,                  ; unix seconds, excluded
}

; The two endpoints section 5.2 names, both inside the signature. Neither is derivable from the items,
; which is the whole reason for carrying them: an anchor a reader invented would let a deployment start
; the walk wherever it liked, and a head read off the last item handed over would let it stop there,
; and a run of receipts the reader was never shown is exactly what a chain on its own cannot see.
; Publishing the head is what makes deletion detectable, and publishing it inside a signature is what
; makes it the deployment's own statement rather than the reader's guess.
;
; Before any retirement the anchor is thirty-two zero bytes, because the head of an empty chain has no
; predecessor; after one it is the `seam` the trim record carries. Both arrive here as the same value,
; and a reader does not have to know which happened in order to start walking.
PackChain = {
    anchor: bstr .size 32,    ; digest the first item was chained from
    head: bstr .size 32,      ; digest of the last item in the chain
}

; The duty this pack answers, and the revision of the mapping it was answered from.
;
; `rev` is the member that makes the other three readable later. The period a duty requires comes out
; of a mapping that changes, and a claim made in 2027 has to be read against the revision it was made
; from rather than against whatever the mapping says on the day somebody opens the artifact. Without
; `rev` a pack dates its own conclusion by its own opening, which is how a format silently rewrites
; the record it was written to hold.
;
; `required` is the deployment's to state and this format supplies no default for it. 19(1) has a floor
; the deployment measures against; 19(2) and 26(6) route a financial institution's logs into the
; applicable Union financial-services law, where the period is longer and is not ours to name. An
; artifact written against a routed article carries the period that law states, and a reader who cannot
; see where the number came from has `rev` to ask it of. `art` is a label from the retention-duty
; registry, which this estate reads as "19(1)", "19(2)" and "26(6)". It is a tstr and not an enum for
; the reason `mk.sch` is in `receipt.cddl`: the reader that knows the registry refuses a label outside
; it, and this file does not decide a question it cannot check.
;
; `held` is not derivable from the items either, which is the only reason it is stated here rather
; than left to a reader: a pack covers one span, the duty runs against everything the store still
; holds, and the oldest receipt in this container need not be the oldest one retained.
;
; `met` is absent on purpose. A reader holding `at`, `held` and `required` computes it in one
; comparison, and what a deployment signs about its own compliance is a conclusion where what it signs
; about the window is evidence. An artifact that states the conclusion as well invites a reader to
; stop checking the numbers.
PackDuty = {
    art: tstr,                ; a label from the retention-duty registry, e.g. "19(1)"
    rev: int,                 ; unix seconds, the revision `required` was read from
    required: int,            ; seconds that revision required
    held: int,                ; seconds the store has held the oldest receipt it retains, at `at`
}

; One receipt, with the three values its record was hashed with and nothing else. These four are the
; whole of what it takes to rebuild the frame: `kind` is 0 for every item, because a trim record
; carries no receipt and is never an item; `idLen` and `len` are counts of the bytes that follow them;
; and the digest the next item names — or the head, for the last one — is sha256 over
; 0x00 || prev || iat as eight big-endian bytes || the byte length of id as two big-endian bytes
; || id || receipt. Every integer in that input is unsigned and big-endian, which is the framing
; section 5.2 publishes, and the `id` bound below is that framing's own: `idLen` is two bytes, and an
; empty id belongs to a trim.
;
; The item's `iat` is the stamp the record was chained with, and it has to equal the `iat` the receipt
; itself attests. They are two statements and the store does not make them one: it chains a receipt
; under the stamp it was handed. A reader who checked the walk and not the equality would accept a
; receipt moved into a window it was never issued in, so the equality is part of the walk and no
; member here stands in for it.
;
; Order in the array bears nothing: the reader follows the `prev` links, because retiring a prefix and
; compacting a file rewrites the front of a chain without touching a surviving record's digest. The
; item whose `prev` is the anchor is first and the item whose own digest is the head is last. Every
; item has to be reached from the anchor and exactly one has to end the walk, which is what makes a
; dropped receipt break a link and a receipt borrowed from elsewhere either unreached or unnamed by
; anybody's `prev`. That is also why no index travels with an item: an ordinal would state a second
; time an order the bytes already fix, and would be free to disagree with it.
;
; `[+ …]` requires at least one item. A pack of nothing has no first node, so its walk is vacuous: the
; reader could check that the head equals the anchor and nothing else, and the artifact would read as
; evidence while proving only that somebody signed two digests. That a span held no receipts at all is
; a statement about the window, and it belongs to the artifact that states windows rather than to the
; container that carries receipts.
PackItem = {
    id: tstr .size (1..65535), ; the store's own id for the receipt, as the record names it
    iat: int,                 ; unix seconds, the stamp the record was chained at
    prev: bstr .size 32,      ; digest of the record before it, or the anchor for the first item
    receipt: bstr .cbor Ashaveri-Receipt, ; the signed receipt bytes, whole and unaltered
}

; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Closure is one rule across this file and not a rule about one map, which is how `receipt.cddl`
; states it and why this file may. No block above carries a `...`, and the absence means there what it
; means there: a member the version named by `v` does not define makes the document invalid rather
; than a document read with the unexpected member dropped. That reaches the manifest, the span, the
; chain, the duty, the item and the signed header alike. The header closes under the stronger of the
; two reasons — its bytes are hashed into the `Sig_structure`, so a label that block does not name is
; an authenticated parameter, and a reader that took its three labels out of a header carrying a fourth
; would hand on a document other than the one the issuer signed — and its members are the COSE
; registry's integers, which is why they are numbered rather than named.
;
; One map a signer fills at will, and that is a decision rather than a gap: the `unprotected` map of
; `COSE_Sign1-Pack-COSE` sits outside the signature, so nothing written in it can travel as a claim
; about the span, and enforcing that it is empty would buy strictness with no security content behind
; it. What the format declares of it is only that it carries no claim.
;
; Every number above written `int` is a CBOR integer, major type 0 or 1, and no other major type
; carries one. A floating-point number whose value happens to be a whole number is not an integer
; written awkwardly: it is a different type, and the only reader that can ever tell them apart is one
; reading the bytes, because a decoder that hands both over as one JavaScript number leaves a check
; placed afterwards nothing to distinguish. So a reader decodes the manifest and the protected header,
; the two maps whose every member is named in this file, where no floating-point number may appear, in
; a value and in a key alike. Nothing either map declares can be a float, so that rule refuses no
; document that follows this file. It matters here more than it might elsewhere: `rev`, `required`,
; `held` and the two span bounds are durations and instants, and a duration is the kind of quantity
; somebody reaches for a fraction of, which is precisely the reading this rule is there to make
; impossible rather than merely unlikely.
; ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
